    "body": "Norfolk Southern’s third-quarter profits rose as higher volume and rates, and fuel surcharges, produced record revenue.\n“Our third quarter was strong with steady execution, making notable improvements to our network while delivering solid results,” Chief Executive Mark George said on the railroad’s earnings call. “We have an outstanding team of thoroughbreds who remain focused on the fundamentals: operating a safe, reliable railroad, serving our customers, growing the business, and improving productivity across the network.”\nOperating income increased 5% to $1.15 billion, as revenue grew 14% to $3.52 billion. Fuel surcharge revenue was responsible for 8 of the 14 points of revenue growth. Earnings per share rose 7%, to $3.37.\nThe railroad’s (NYSE: NSC ) operating ratio was 67.4%, up 2.8 points from a year ago largely due to higher fuel costs. Adjusted for the impact of ongoing costs related to the February 2023 derailment and hazardous materials release in East Palestine, Ohio, and expenses related to the proposed merger with Union Pacific (NYSE: UNP ), the operating ratio was 65.4%.\nOverall volume was up 3% for the quarter, with merchandise, intermodal , and coal volume all up 3%.\n“Demand for rail service is growing with encouraging trends in areas such as domestic intermodal and chemicals,” George said. “The current macro environment is tightening the truck market, and that helps rail be more competitive. And what you saw in the quarter is us capturing the increased customer demand.”\nChief Commercial Officer Ed Elkins said NS is broadly optimistic for the rest of 2026. High fuel prices tied to the Middle East conflict are boosting energy-related traffic. But NS is watching whether prolonged high gas prices eventually soften consumer demand.\nA tight truck market and high fuel costs favor highway-to-rail conversions in forest products, consumer products, and domestic intermodal, Elkins said. International intermodal remains weighed down by trade uncertainty. Manufacturing has expanded nine straight months, industrial development is ahead of last year, and vehicle production looks modestly positive. Coal is expected to grow, led by exports and supported by utilities replenishing depleted stockpiles.\nNS has rolled out new online customer tools that speed responses to rate requests and provide better shipment visibility.\n“The result is more accurate and more actionable shipment information, enabling customers to plan more effectively, operate more efficiently, and ultimately make decisions with greater confidence,” Elkins said. “As these capabilities continue to scale, our goal is simple: Make Norfolk Southern the easiest railroad to do business with so that we win more freight in competitive markets.”\nKey operational metrics were down compared to a year ago but improved over the second quarter, when spot crew shortages led to congestion. Terminal dwell improved 3% and train speed was up 5% compared to the second quarter. On-time train originations improved 18%.\n“Our railroaders managed another demanding quarter, overcoming significant weather and operating disruptions while remaining focused on serving our customers and operating safely,” Chief Operating Officer Brian Barr said.\nThe intermodal service composite was 84%, down from 92% a year ago. Merchandise trip plan compliance was 74%, down from 80% a year ago. Compared to the second quarter, merchandise plan compliance rose 6 points, while the intermodal metric declined by a point.\n“We entered the quarter with a clear plan to recover network fluidity by improving originations, reducing dwell, increasing velocity, and getting the railroad back on schedule,” Barr said. “We delivered sequential improvement across those foundational measures.”\nThe improvements in the quarter came despite weather-related disruptions in August.\n“On our network’s high-speed corridor, the team quickly restored infrastructure, removing hundreds of downed trees, repairing multiple washouts, and handling widespread power outages by activating strategic reroutes and repositioning resources,” Barr said. “While the historic wind and rain interrupted some of our July momentum, it did not derail our progress.”\nOne way NS reduced dwell was by redesigning car connections at terminals, which eliminated handlings. The changes removed 95 crew starts per week and took 200 cars per day off the network, while the railroad absorbed 3% carload growth, Barr said.\nNS also consolidated smaller trains between Birmingham, Ala., and Macon, Ga., saving 14 crew starts per week while reducing locomotive needs. Shifting 50 daily switch cars from Enola, Pa., to Allentown Yard freed up Enola crews for road service and moved the switching to a yard with a lower cost per car. It also opens up yard capacity in Enola, Barr said.\nThe railroad’s personal injury and train accident rates continued to show improvement through the first nine months of the year. The train accident rate was at a record low for the railroad.\nSubscribe to FreightWaves’ Rail e-newsletter and get the latest insights on rail freight right in your inbox.\nRead more:\nCommodity markets whip up railfreight gains\nFirst look: Union Pacific earnings\nFirst look: Norfolk Southern earnings\nCSX profit jumps on volume and revenue growth\nNew York short line wins $11.2 million federal rail grant\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
